---
import FacebookPost from "./FacebookPost.astro";
import Icon from "./Icon.astro";
import { facebookPosts, pageName, pageAvatar } from "../data/facebook";
import { facebook } from "../data/contact";

/**
 * "Co u nas słychać" - the two most recent Facebook posts, first thing under the hero.
 *
 * The site is a catalogue that changes once a season. Nothing on it could say *chrysanthemums
 * are on sale now*, and the owners already write exactly that on Facebook. This block is that
 * writing, and it exists so they do not have to do it twice. It moved to the top of the home
 * page in September 2026 on the owner's instruction, for that reason: it is the only thing on
 * the page that can differ between two Tuesdays.
 *
 * The card itself is `FacebookPost.astro`, reproduced from the sibling site
 * alpaki-kazimierzdolny.pl in the same change. This file is the band around it - the heading,
 * the lead, the row of cards and the way out.
 *
 * ## What this is not
 *
 * It is not an embed. There is no Facebook script, no iframe, no SDK, no like button and no
 * `fbcdn.net` in the built page - the text is data in the repository and the photographs and
 * films are files in `src/assets/facebook/`, put there by `scripts/fetch-facebook.mjs` and
 * optimised by the same pipeline as every other picture here. **That is why this block, alone
 * among the third-party things on this site, needs no consent gate:** the visitor's browser
 * never contacts Meta. The only link out is one they choose to click. If anybody ever
 * "simplifies" this into a plugin or a hotlinked `full_picture`, that property is what gets
 * thrown away - and § 5 of the privacy policy becomes false with it.
 *
 * ## Rules that are easy to break here
 *
 * - **The text is not rewritten.** Same rule as the plant descriptions: it is the owners'
 *   voice, including any emoji they used. It is not shortened either any more - the four-line
 *   clamp is the browser's and only applies where scripting is on, so the whole post is always
 *   in the document. The old 200-character cut sent people to Meta to read a sentence we had
 *   and withheld.
 * - **No phone number.** The home page prints a number exactly twice (Intro, footer) and
 *   `src/pages/index.astro` says to count it on the built page. This block adds none.
 * - **Two cards, and the number was argued rather than inherited.** It was three until
 *   September 2026 - the sibling site shows three and the fetch script kept three - and the
 *   owner asked whether three was right, having noticed that the home page carries a run of
 *   trios. It does, and the ones that matter are adjacent: this row and the season calendar
 *   under it have the same anatomy (uppercase label, heading, paragraph, photograph, a small
 *   line beneath), so two identical grids of three sat one on top of the other.
 *
 *   What settled it was not the rhythm but the date. The card prints "3 dni temu", and **that**
 *   is what says the holding is alive - not the count. A third post therefore buys redundancy,
 *   not freshness, and it costs the widest thing on the page: at three columns a card is about
 *   340px and the four-line clamp cuts nearly every post the owners write, where at two it is
 *   about 540px and most of them fit whole.
 *
 *   **The argument that lost is worth keeping**, because it is the one that will come back: a
 *   post with no text, or a share of somebody else's, is ordinary on Facebook, and at three
 *   cards one dud is a third of the section while at two it is half. If the feed turns out to
 *   be full of them, that is the reason to go back to three - not the rhythm.
 * - **An empty snapshot still renders the section**, with a different lead and no cards. That
 *   is where this parts company with the sibling site, which hides the block entirely. It is
 *   the owners' call: they wanted a block that is always on the page, saying where the news
 *   lives. It invents no news - the empty lead says only that they post on Facebook and that
 *   new posts land here, and it keeps the link the populated state also carries.
 *
 *   The same state covers a snapshot that has gone stale; see MAX_AGE_DAYS in
 *   `src/data/facebook.ts` for why an old post is treated as no post.
 *
 *   **Both leads, the overline and the heading are our words, not the owners'**, and are on
 *   their review list in docs/inwentaryzacja.md together. The populated one was cut to its
 *   first clause in September 2026 on the owner's instruction - it used to carry "- co właśnie
 *   kwitnie i co jest w sprzedaży", which the posts underneath say better than a lead can.
 */

/** Two is the whole row - see the note above. `scripts/fetch-facebook.mjs` keeps exactly two,
 *  so this slice is the belt to that braces. */
const posts = facebookPosts.slice(0, 2);
---

<section id="aktualnosci" class="news" aria-labelledby="aktualnosci-title">
  <div class="news__head">
    <p class="eyebrow news__eyebrow">Bądź na bieżąco</p>
    <h2 class="news__title" id="aktualnosci-title">Co u nas słychać</h2>
    <p class="news__lead">
      {
        posts.length > 0
          ? "Ostatnie wpisy z naszego profilu na Facebooku."
          : "Najświeższe wiadomości piszemy na naszym profilu na Facebooku - co właśnie kwitnie i co jest w sprzedaży. Gdy pojawi się tam nowy wpis, trafia także tutaj."
      }
    </p>
  </div>

  {
    posts.length > 0 && (
      /* The column count follows the number of posts rather than being written down as a
         number, and that is a fix rather than a flourish: `KEEP` is what the fetch script tries
         for, not what it gets - a post with neither text nor a photograph is dropped, so the
         feed can hand back one post where two were asked for. With the count hard-coded, that
         left a card sitting against the left edge and an empty column beside it. */
      <div
        class:list={["news__grid", { "news__grid--single": posts.length === 1 }]}
        style={`--cols: ${posts.length}`}
      >
        {posts.map((post) => (
          <FacebookPost post={post} pageName={pageName} avatar={pageAvatar} />
        ))}
      </div>
    )
  }

  <p class:list={["news__cta", { "news__cta--alone": posts.length === 0 }]}>
    <a class="cta news__button" href={facebook} target="_blank" rel="noopener">
      <Icon name="facebook" />
      Obserwuj nas na Facebooku
    </a>
  </p>
</section>

<script>
  import "../scripts/facebook-news";
</script>

<style>
  /* **No `border-top` here, and that is a fix rather than an omission.** `Intro.astro` already
     closes itself with a `border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule)`, and when this section moved up
     under the hero in September 2026 the two met with nothing between them: two 1px rules
     stacked, drawn as a single 2px line, on a site whose whole vocabulary for separation is the
     1px rule. It read as a defect because it was one. The hero's own line does the parting. */
  .news {
    background: var(--paper-clay);
    padding: var(--pad-section) var(--edge);
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--gap-entry);
  }

  /* The rule under the heading is the one every other section on this site draws, with the
     same weight, the same colour and the same 0.9rem of air above it - see `.overview__head`,
     `.show__head` and `.comp__head`. It was missing here, which made this the only band whose
     heading ran straight into its contents. */
  .news__head {
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.9rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
    padding-bottom: 0.9rem;
  }

  .news__eyebrow {
    font-size: 0.72rem;
  }

  .news__title {
    font-size: var(--text-h2);
  }

  .news__lead {
    max-width: 46ch;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }

  /* One column, and one per post from the tablet width up - `--cols` is set on the element
     from `posts.length`, so a feed that yields one post gets one column rather than one card
     and a hole.

     This is the project's one deliberate breakpoint outside `prefers-reduced-motion`, and it is
     not a layout that could have been written in `auto-fit` - it is the condition the four
     shared rows below depend on. `subgrid` only means anything where the cards stand side by
     side; stacked, there is nothing to line up with, and the four-line reservation that stands
     in for it would be four lines of nothing under every short post. An `auto-fit` grid cannot
     say which of the two it is in. */
  .news__grid {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--gap-cards);
    grid-template-columns: 1fr;
  }

  @media (min-width: 700px) {
    .news__grid {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(var(--cols, 2), minmax(0, 1fr));
    }

    /* **One post must not take the whole shell**, and this is the second half of the fix
       above rather than a flourish. Removing the empty column left the opposite defect: a
       single card 1139px wide sets the owners' text in lines of over a hundred characters
       and blows a 4:3 frame up to about 790px tall - a photograph bigger than the hero's.
       36rem is about what a card gets when there are two of them, so the card looks the
       same whether the feed gave one post or two, which is the point. */
    .news__grid--single {
      max-width: 36rem;
    }
  }

  /* Four named rows - header, text, photographs, footer - that every card adopts with
     `subgrid`, so the cards line up row by row however long their posts happen to be. The row
     gap belongs to the card at that point, not to the grid between cards, which is why it drops
     to zero here and the gap between columns is set on its own. The cards make one row, so
     nothing needs a gap between rows of cards. */
  @supports (grid-template-rows: subgrid) {
    @media (min-width: 700px) {
      .news__grid {
        grid-template-rows: auto auto auto auto;
        column-gap: var(--gap-cards);
        row-gap: 0;
      }

      /* Set by src/scripts/facebook-news.ts while at least one card is open, and taken off
         when the last one closes - a count, not a switch.
         Rows shared between cards cannot grow for one of them: expanding a post in place would
         push the one beside it open by the same amount, and a post with no text at all is
         ordinary here, so its neighbour would be holding several centimetres of nothing above
         its photograph. That is the hole `subgrid` was brought in to close. So while a
         post is open the row lets go instead: each card takes its own height, the photographs
         stop lining up, and nothing is padded out. The alignment comes back with the last card
         that closes. */
      .news__grid[data-loose] {
        grid-template-rows: none;
        align-items: start;
      }
    }
  }

  .news__cta {
    display: grid;
    justify-items: center;
    margin: 0;
  }

  /* Centred under three cards, which is what it is centred against. With no cards there is
     nothing above it to be centred under, and the button drifts into the middle of an empty
     band while the heading and the lead sit hard left. So the empty state puts it back under
     the sentence it belongs to. The sibling site never faces this - there the whole section
     disappears instead. */
  .news__cta--alone {
    justify-items: start;
  }

  /* `.cta` from global.css carries the shape - the one filled block on this site, deep green
     with paper type. Only the glyph and its gap are local. Deliberately in the site's green
     rather than Facebook's blue; the blue appears once, in the mark in the corner of each
     card. */
  .news__button {
    gap: 0.6rem;
    justify-content: center;
  }
  .news__button :global(.icon) {
    width: 1.05em;
    height: 1.05em;
  }

  /* On a phone the button takes the column: a centred block with the screen's width to spare
     on either side reads as an afterthought. */
  @media (max-width: 520px) {
    .news__cta {
      justify-items: stretch;
    }
  }
</style>
